          "id": "l17",
          "speaker": "trainer",
          "text": "This is not a story. In 1962, two mathematicians, David Gale and Lloyd Shapley, worked out how people pair up.",
          "pauseAfterMs": 500
        },
        {
          "id": "l18",
          "speaker": "trainer",
          "text": "They proved the side that asks gets the best partner it could *keep.*",
          "say": "They proved, the side that asks gets the best partner it could keep.",
          "pauseAfterMs": 500
        },
        {
          "id": "l19",
          "speaker": "trainer",
          "text": "The side that waits gets the *worst* partner who would still stay.",
          "say": "The side that waits, gets the worst partner who would still stay.",
          "pauseAfterMs": 900
        },
        {
          "id": "l20",
          "speaker": "trainer",
          "text": "In 2012, that work won a *Nobel Prize.*",
          "pauseAfterMs": 600
        },
        {
          "id": "l21",
          "speaker": "trainer",
          "text": "This year, that same method placed more than thirty-eight thousand young doctors in US hospitals.",
          "pauseAfterMs": 500
        },
        {
          "id": "l22",
          "speaker": "trainer",
          "text": "There, the *doctors* do the asking.",
          "pauseAfterMs": 800
        },
        {
          "id": "l23",
          "speaker": "trainer",
          "text": "You do this too, waiting for the text, the call, the *first move.*",
